{"causes":["The Zhou Dynasty's emphasis on education and literature led to the compilation of the Classic of Poetry.","The Chinese Civil War (475–221 BCE) resulted in the collection of poems from various regions, which were later compiled into the Classic of Poetry.","The introduction of Confucianism as a state philosophy during the Han Dynasty (206 BCE–220 CE) solidified the Classic of Poetry's position as a foundational text."],"effects":["The Classic of Poetry has had a lasting impact on Chinese literature and culture, influencing generations of poets and thinkers.","The poem 'Li Sao' continues to be widely studied and admired today.","The Classic of Poetry has inspired numerous adaptations, including films, plays, and musical compositions that have been performed around the world."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Zhou_Dynasty","sourceName":"Zhou Dynasty","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"compiled by scholars during the Zhou Dynasty (1046–256 BCE)"},{"sourceSlug":"Wen_Yao","sourceName":"Wen Yao","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"one of the legendary poets whose works were included in the collection"},{"sourceSlug":"Han_Dynasty","sourceName":"Han Dynasty","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"the Confucian state philosophy during this period solidified the collection's position as a foundational text"},{"sourceSlug":"Matsuo_Bashó","sourceName":"Matsuo Bashō","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"Japanese literature, particularly his works, were influenced by the Classic of Poetry"},{"sourceSlug":"China","sourceName":"China","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"the collection is a cornerstone of Chinese literature and culture"},{"sourceSlug":"Confucianism","sourceName":"Confucianism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"the state philosophy during this period solidified the collection's position as a foundational text"},{"sourceSlug":"Chinese_Civil_War","sourceName":"Chinese Civil War","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"Classic_of_Poetry","targetName":"The Classic of Poetry","context":"the collection of poems from various regions was gathered and compiled during this period"},{"sourceSlug":"Chu_Fu","sourceName":"Chu Fu","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Li_Sao","targetName":"Li Sao","context":"'Ode to Spring' is one of the most famous lines from the Classic of Poetry"}],"places":[{"name":"Zhou_Dynasty","role":"Compilation Site"},{"name":"Han_Dynasty","role":"Influence Period"},{"name":"China","role":"Origin"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Classic of Poetry","type":"Reference article","slug":"classic-of-poetry"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q465108","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classic_of_Poetry"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Classic of Poetry left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 53 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Classic of Poetry (Chinese: 詩經, Shījīng, 'Book of Songs' or 'Book of Odes') is the oldest existing collection of Chinese poetry, comprising 305 poems dated to the Western Zhou and early Eastern Zhou dynasties (c. 1046–600 BCE), and one of the Five Classics of the Confucian literary canon. It encompasses a remarkable range of poetic forms and themes: folk songs (guofeng, 'airs of the states') from fifteen different regions describing everyday life, love, courtship, farming, and seasonal change; court hymns (ya) used in aristocratic ceremonies; and ritual hymns (song) used in ancestral worship. Confucius himself reportedly edited the Classic of Poetry and attached great importance to it — the Analects records him saying 'If a man has not studied the Odes, he will be at a loss to express himself' — and the canonical status the Confucian tradition gave the collection ensured that Chinese literary culture and the Chinese classical examination system took it as their foundational poetic text for over two millennia.

The Classic of Poetry's influence on Chinese literary tradition is comparable to Homer's on the Greek tradition and the Bible's on Western medieval literature — it established the forms, themes, and allusive tradition within which all subsequent Chinese poetry was written, and a sophisticated literary-critical tradition of interpreting the Odes developed over centuries. The Confucian hermeneutical tradition interpreted even the apparently simple love songs as allegorical expressions of political loyalty and moral relationships — the 'fishhawk' poem that opens the collection ('Guan guan goes the fishhawk, on the islet in the river; beautiful is this noble lady, a good mate for the lord') was read as an allegory of the king seeking virtuous ministers. This allegorical tradition shaped the entire Chinese classical literary culture and its approach to reading literary texts as expressions of moral and political truth.

The Classic of Poetry also played a significant role in women's literary culture in imperial China — its representation of female voices (the folk songs include poems from women's perspectives on love, abandonment, and the hardships of marriage) and the female poets of the Tang dynasty who wrote in its tradition made it one of the few canonical texts in which women's voices were represented.","subjects":["Chinese Literature","Classical Era","Confucianism","Chinese History","Poetry","East Asia","Zhou Dynasty","Imperial Examination"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Classic of Poetry has had a profound impact on Chinese literature and culture, influencing generations of poets and thinkers.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
